Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether, for **any** sequence of random variables $Y_n$, there exist deterministic constants $d_n \to 0$ with $d_n Y_n \to 0$ almost surely.

**Answer: Yes.**

---

## Proof

**Key observation.** Each $Y_n$ is a real-valued random variable, so $|Y_n| < \infty$ a.s., which gives $\lim_{t\to\infty} P(|Y_n| > t) = 0$ for every $n$.

**Construction of $c_n$ (with $d_n = 1/c_n$).** For each $n \geq 1$ and each $k \geq 1$, since $P(|Y_n| > t) \to 0$ as $t \to \infty$, we can pick a threshold $t_{n,k} > 0$ such that
$$P(|Y_n| > t_{n,k}) < \frac{1}{n^2 \, 2^k}.$$
Now define
$$c_n = \max\!\Big(n,\;\max_{1 \le k \le n} k \cdot t_{n,k}\Big), \qquad d_n = \frac{1}{c_n}.$$

**$d_n \to 0$.** Since $c_n \ge n$, we have $c_n \to \infty$, hence $d_n \to 0$. ✓

**Summability.** For any fixed $k \ge 1$ and all $n \ge k$, we have $c_n \ge k \cdot t_{n,k}$, so $c_n/k \ge t_{n,k}$, and therefore
$$P\!\left(|Y_n| > \frac{c_n}{k}\right) \le P(|Y_n| > t_{n,k}) < \frac{1}{n^2\, 2^k}.$$
Summing over $n \ge k$:
$$\sum_{n=k}^{\infty} P\!\left(|Y_n| > \frac{c_n}{k}\right) \le \frac{1}{2^k}\sum_{n=k}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^2} < \infty.$$

**Borel–Cantelli (first lemma, no independence required).** For each $k \ge 1$:
$$P\!\left(|Y_n| > \frac{c_n}{k}\ \text{i.o.}\right) = 0.$$
Equivalently, defining $A_k = \bigl\{\omega : |Y_n(\omega)| \le c_n/k \text{ for all sufficiently large } n\bigr\}$, we have $P(A_k) = 1$.

**Almost sure convergence.** Set $A = \bigcap_{k=1}^{\infty} A_k$. Then $P(A) = 1$. On $A$, for every $k \ge 1$ there exists $N_k(\omega)$ such that for all $n \ge N_k(\omega)$:
$$|d_n\, Y_n(\omega)| = \frac{|Y_n(\omega)|}{c_n} \le \frac{1}{k}.$$
This is precisely the statement that $d_n\, Y_n(\omega) \to 0$. Since this holds on $A$ with $P(A)=1$:
$$d_n\, Y_n \to 0 \quad \text{a.s.}$$

**Conclusion.** For every sequence of (real-valued) random variables $Y_n$, the deterministic constants $d_n = 1/c_n$ constructed above satisfy $d_n \to 0$ and $d_n Y_n \to 0$ almost surely. The only property needed is that each $Y_n$ is finite almost surely (so that its tail probabilities vanish), and the first Borel–Cantelli lemma requires no independence assumption. $\blacksquare$

